(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pulmonary-nodules-neoplasms-vignettes"]={"title":"Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has a biopsy-proven 2.8-cm non-small cell carcinoma in the right upper lobe. Positron emission tomography shows uptake in a hilar lymph node on the same side, but no mediastinal or other nodes are involved, and there is no distant metastasis. Which stage is her cancer?","choices":["Stage 1A","Stage 2A","Stage 2B","Stage 3A"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A tumor no more than 5 cm that has spread to the peribronchial, hilar or intrapulmonary nodes is stage 2B. Her 2.8-cm tumor would be stage 1A without nodes, but the involved same-side hilar node, scored N1, moves it to stage 2B; mediastinal nodes would have made it stage 3. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 25, 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a biopsy-proven 3.5-cm lung adenocarcinoma. Computed tomography has shown its size and location. Which study in her staging workup looks for lymph node involvement?","choices":["Repeat biopsy of the primary","Pulmonary function testing","Chest radiograph","Positron emission tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lung cancer is staged once computed tomography, positron emission tomography and biopsy results are back: computed tomography identifies location and size, positron emission tomography looks at lymph involvement, and biopsy classes the tumor type. Tumor size, node involvement and distant metastasis then set the stage. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 22"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a 50 pack-year history is diagnosed with small cell lung carcinoma after presenting with bone pain. Computed tomography shows a mass beside the left hilum. His wife asks where this cancer usually arises and why it was not caught earlier. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Central; often spread at diagnosis","Peripheral; easy to find early","Central; rarely spreads early","Peripheral; caught by sputum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma has a central location and infiltrates the mucosa, so it develops insidiously, and over 70% of patients are metastatic, to the liver, bone or brain, before detection. His hilar mass and bone pain fit that pattern: central, and already spread when found. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman who still smokes and has a 50 pack-year history has had 6 weeks of hemoptysis and has lost 4 kg. She saw an advertisement and asks to enroll in a lung cancer screening program. Which plan should be avoided?","choices":["Enrolling her in annual screening","Contrast chest computed tomography","Biopsy of any lesion found","Metabolic panel with calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening is for high-risk individuals without symptoms or a known nodule. Hemoptysis and weight loss are symptoms that should be worked up for possible lung cancer now, with computed tomography with contrast of the chest and upper abdomen followed by biopsy, so screening would delay her diagnosis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 14, 18"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has an image-guided biopsy confirming non-small cell carcinoma in a 1.8-cm peripheral nodule, with no lymph node or distant spread. Her surgeon judges her fit for an operation, but her pulmonary function tests show she would not tolerate losing a whole lobe. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Anatomical segmentectomy","Lobectomy","Pneumonectomy","Chemotherapy alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When a fit patient's image-guided biopsy confirms malignancy, the options are lobectomy or, if the patient is unfit for lobectomy, an anatomical segmentectomy. A segment resection removes a larger portion of the lobe than a wedge but less than a lobectomy, and the operation must be one she can survive and recover from. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 35"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man who smokes has a bronchial biopsy showing dysplasia: the cells have changed form but have not invaded the surrounding tissue. He asks what would come next if these cells keep progressing. Which change comes next?","choices":["In situ cancer","Hyperplasia","Invasion of blood and lymph","Distant metastases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lung cancer develops in a sequence: a genetically altered epithelial cell, hyperplasia (cells dividing faster than normal), dysplasia (cells changing form), in situ cancer (cells staying in one place), then a malignant tumor whose cells invade normal tissue and enter blood and lymph, and finally distant metastases. After dysplasia comes in situ cancer. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man who still smokes and has a 60 pack-year history has a new, solid 22-mm nodule in the left upper lobe with a spiculated (spiky) border. His primary care clinician judges the probability of malignancy to be high. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Hospice and palliative care","Annual screening program","Smoking cessation program","Thoracic surgeon"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A high probability of malignancy in a new solid nodule of 8 to 30 mm leads to standard stage evaluation and, when no metastases are found, video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery with resection, and referral to a subspecialist such as a thoracic surgeon is recommended. His upper-lobe location, spiky border and heavy smoking all add to the concern. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 16, 18"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with severe heart failure is unfit for surgery. Computed tomography shows a growing 2.5-cm nodule lying against a large blood vessel, and the interventional radiologist judges image-guided biopsy unsafe. Positron emission tomography shows intense uptake in the nodule and no lymph node or distant spread. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Image-guided biopsy despite risk","Wedge resection with frozen section","Repeat imaging in a year","Radiotherapy without biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For a patient unfit for surgery, image-guided biopsy is the next step only if it is possible and safe. When biopsy is not possible or not safe, the pathway goes straight to stereotactic ablative radiotherapy, radiofrequency ablation or conventional radical radiotherapy, so her unsafe biopsy does not block treatment. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 17"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with limited-stage small cell lung carcinoma has a complete response to initial chemotherapy and thoracic radiation. Her cognition is normal and she is active and independent. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Observation alone","Lobectomy","Routine positron emission tomography","Prophylactic cranial irradiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After initial therapy, limited-stage small cell carcinoma with a complete or partial response goes on to prophylactic cranial irradiation, radiating the brain to prevent metastasis. Her normal cognition and good function leave no reason to withhold it. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 41, 43"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with a biopsy-proven 4-cm non-small cell carcinoma has a staging scan showing metastases in the right adrenal gland and in several vertebrae. Which stage is her cancer?","choices":["Stage 3B","Stage 3C","Stage 4A","Stage 4B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Multiple extrathoracic metastases, in one or more organs, are scored M1c, and spread to multiple sites outside the chest, such as the adrenal gland and bones, is stage 4B. Any tumor size and any node status still give stage 4B once the cancer has reached several sites beyond the chest. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 28, 29"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with a 35 pack-year smoking history is diagnosed with lung cancer. She asks how cigarette smoke turned normal lung cells into cancer. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Oncogenes on, suppressor genes off","Suppressor genes on, oncogenes off","Cells become more differentiated","Smoke causes a lung infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Carcinogens, or other causes of genetic mutation, activate oncogenes in lung cells, and further mutations or loss of regulatory control inactivate tumor suppressor genes. Atypical cells then proliferate locally and spread, becoming poorly differentiated as the cancer grows and drives angiogenesis to exceed its boundaries. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with lung adenocarcinoma has metastases in the liver, an adrenal gland and several bones, placing him at stage 4B. Which treatment is standard for his cancer?","choices":["Chemotherapy","Lobectomy","Pneumonectomy","Observation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For non-small cell lung cancer, stage 4 is treated with chemotherapy, with surgical resection considered only for a solitary metastatic lesion with a resectable primary tumor. Chemotherapy and radiation are used as adjuncts to surgery or when the patient is stage 4. He has metastases at several sites, so resection is not the plan. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 35"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a biopsy-proven large cell carcinoma in the periphery of the right lung. Her oncologist warns that this tumor type can spread to a site that is unusual for lung cancers. Which site is it?","choices":["Spine","Small bowel","Liver","Brain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Large cell carcinoma metastasizes to rare areas such as the small intestine and bowel. Lung cancers in general spread to regional lymph nodes and then to the liver, brain and bone, so those sites are expected rather than unusual. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 33"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a 60 pack-year history is brought to the emergency department with new confusion, an unsteady gait and a seizure. Serum sodium is 118 mEq/L (normal 135-145) and serum osmolality is 250 mOsm/kg (normal 275-295). He has no peripheral edema and no signs of pulmonary edema. Computed tomography shows a central hilar mass. Which lung cancer type is most likely?","choices":["Adenocarcinoma","Bronchial carcinoid","Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Small cell lung cancer can produce the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone through its neuroendocrine cells, causing excess vasopressin and water retention. The result is hyponatremia and low serum osmolality, here 118 and 250, with confusion, seizures and ataxia but no peripheral or pulmonary edema, because the process is insidious. The central mass fits too. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 31, 41, 46, 48"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man who smokes 2 packs a day is worried after his brother was diagnosed with lung cancer. He asks which type of lung cancer is most strongly associated with smoking. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Bronchial carcinoid","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma, the second most common lung cancer, has the strongest association with smoking. Smoking is implicated in 85 to 90% of all lung cancers and compounds with packs per day and years, but among the cell types it is squamous cell carcinoma that is singled out as most strongly tied to smoking. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 4, 31, 33, 39, 48"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a 40 pack-year history was diagnosed with peripheral lung adenocarcinoma after months of cough and chest tightness. He asks which new symptoms would suggest the cancer has advanced. Which findings would signal later disease?","choices":["Hemoptysis and fatigue","Small bowel metastases","Bone pain and weight loss","Cough and chest tightness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adenocarcinoma is mostly asymptomatic early and usually presents first with respiratory symptoms: cough, shortness of breath, chest tightness or pain, hemoptysis and fatigue. Later signs are bone pain, weight loss and other systemic, paraneoplastic issues, and two thirds of patients present only once the disease is advanced or metastatic. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 33"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with active pulmonary sarcoidosis and a 30 pack-year history has a new 16-mm solid nodule. Positron emission tomography shows high uptake in the nodule. He asks whether this result proves he has cancer. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure: it is inflammation","Annual screening scans","Tissue biopsy of the nodule","Start chemotherapy now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Positron emission tomography uses a radioactive sugar that highly metabolic cells take up, suggesting malignancy, but high uptake does not rule out inflammatory conditions, which may also take it up. With an active inflammatory lung disease his uptake cannot prove cancer, so tissue is needed; moderate or intense uptake leads to biopsy or resection. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 17, 18"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a 40 pack-year history has a new, solid 18-mm nodule. Her clinician judged the probability of malignancy low to moderate, and positron emission tomography now shows intense uptake in the nodule. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Annual low-dose screening","Repeat positron emission tomography","Biopsy or thoracoscopic resection","Surveillance computed tomography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Moderate or intense uptake on fludeoxyglucose positron emission tomography moves a low-to-moderate probability nodule to nonsurgical biopsy, by transthoracic needle or endoscopic approach, or to video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery with resection, with referral to a subspecialist such as a pulmonologist, thoracic surgeon, interventional radiologist or oncologist. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 17, 18"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with a 40 pack-year history is diagnosed with metastatic lung cancer after presenting with bone pain and weight loss. She never had a cough. Her son asks why the cancer was not found until it had spread. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Screening misses most cancers","Symptoms begin late, when advanced","Most tumors start in the periphery","It rarely spreads before symptoms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. What makes lung cancer so deadly is that patients usually have no symptoms until the disease is advanced or already metastatic. Patients may have no respiratory symptoms at all and present instead with complaints from metastasis, such as bone pain, so her bone pain and weight loss were late signs of silent growth. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 13, 18, 31, 41"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman who has never smoked has had a persistent cough for 3 months and has now coughed up blood twice. A chest film shows a 3-cm mass. A friend told her that lung cancer does not happen at her age. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Most cases occur under 50","It is uncommon but possible","It does not occur under 40","It requires a smoking history"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most lung cancers occur in people older than 50, but about 4% occur in people under 40, so her age does not remove it from the differential. Persistent cough and hemoptysis are symptoms that should be worked up for possible lung cancer, and smoking, though implicated in 85 to 90% of cases, is not required. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 12"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man who quit smoking 30 years ago has an 8-mm nodule on computed tomography done for a rib fracture. It is a smoothly marginated solid nodule with dense calcification in its center and no fat. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Linear scar","Healed granuloma","Hamartoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A smoothly marginated solid nodule with central calcification is typical of a healed granuloma, an inflammatory nodule and one of the main examples of benign nodules. Central calcification is a benign pattern, whereas eccentric or stippled ground-glass calcification is a malignant pattern. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 16, 21"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman who quit smoking 30 years ago has a 2.4-cm spiculated (spiky) nodule in the periphery of the left upper lobe that involves the pleura. Biopsy shows a non-small cell carcinoma. Which stain result would support adenocarcinoma?","choices":["Small cells without nucleoli","Large anaplastic cells","Mucin on mucicarmine stain","Negative mucicarmine stain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Histology may include a mucicarmine stain: mucin production is related to adenocarcinoma, while negative staining may point to large cell carcinoma. A suspicious solid spiculated nodule in the periphery that involves the pleura is the picture of an invasive adenocarcinoma, which grows from type II pneumocytes. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 34, 42"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a 50 pack-year history has a new, solid 25-mm nodule in the right upper lobe with a spiculated (spiky) border. She has no symptoms. Her clinician judges the probability of malignancy to be high. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Surveillance computed tomography","Annual low-dose screening","Repeat chest radiograph","Standard staging evaluation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For a new solid nodule of 8 to 30 mm with a high probability of malignancy, the next step is standard stage evaluation, with or without fludeoxyglucose positron emission tomography. If no metastases are identified, video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery with resection follows. Her upper-lobe location, spiky border and heavy smoking support the high estimate. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 14, 16"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with small cell lung carcinoma diagnosed 2 months ago has a dull ache under his right ribs, poor appetite and yellowing of his eyes. Exam shows an enlarged liver with a firm edge. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Bone metastasis","Brain metastasis","Adrenal metastasis","Liver metastasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma is metastatic, to the liver, bone or brain, before detection in over 70% of patients. Liver metastasis causes a dull right upper quadrant ache, hepatomegaly with a firm edge, lack of appetite, jaundice and eventually hepatic failure, matching his findings. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 13, 41"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a 50 pack-year history has a central lung mass, and biopsy shows small cell carcinoma. Over 2 months he has developed progressive muscle weakness, worst in his legs. Testing confirms Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (autoimmune muscle weakness, particularly in the legs). He asks what causes the weakness. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Low sodium weakens the muscles","Antibodies destroy muscle fibers","Nerve calcium channels are attacked","Tumor compresses the spinal cord"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome is a rare autoimmune disorder in which the immune system attacks calcium channels on nerve endings at the neuromuscular junction. The non-functioning channels prevent release of acetylcholine, so the muscles cannot contract properly, producing progressive weakness, particularly in the legs. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 13, 41, 46, 47, 48"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who has never smoked and has no other lung cancer risk factors has a single, solid 7-mm nodule found incidentally on computed tomography after a car crash. Using the Fleischner Society guidance for incidental solid nodules, which follow-up is recommended?","choices":["Optional computed tomography at 12 months","Computed tomography at 3 months","Computed tomography at 6-12 months","No routine follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For a single solid nodule of 6 to 8 mm in a low-risk patient, the Fleischner Society table gives computed tomography at 6 to 12 months, then considering another scan at 18 to 24 months. He has never smoked and has no other risk factors, so he is low risk. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a 45 pack-year smoking history, which makes her high risk, has two solid nodules of 6 mm and 7 mm found incidentally on computed tomography done for abdominal pain. Using the Fleischner Society guidance for incidental solid nodules, which follow-up is recommended?","choices":["No routine follow-up","Optional computed tomography at 12 months","Computed tomography at 6-12 months","Computed tomography at 3-6 months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For multiple solid nodules of 6 to 8 mm in a high-risk patient, the Fleischner Society table gives computed tomography at 3 to 6 months, then again at 18 to 24 months, using the most suspicious nodule to guide management. Her heavy smoking history makes her high risk. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has a biopsy-proven 3.6-cm non-small cell carcinoma in the left lower lobe. Staging shows no lymph node involvement and no distant metastasis. Which stage is his cancer?","choices":["Stage 1A","Stage 1B","Stage 2A","Stage 3A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A tumor more than 3 cm but no more than 4 cm in its greatest dimension, with no lymph node spread and no metastasis, is stage 1B. His 3.6-cm tumor with clear nodes sits in that band; at this stage the tumor may or may not involve the main bronchus or the lung's inner lining. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a 50 pack-year history has a large, cavitated mass in the periphery of the left upper lobe. Weeks later he develops crampy abdominal pain, and imaging shows a metastasis in the small intestine. Biopsy of the lung mass shows undifferentiated cells about three times normal size that do not stain for mucin. Which lung cancer type is most likely?","choices":["Large cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Large cell carcinoma forms in the periphery, shows a large cavitated mass on computed tomography, and has anaplastic, undifferentiated cells about 3 times larger than baseline that lack specific markers. Negative mucicarmine staining may point to it, and its spread to unusual sites such as the small intestine and bowel is characteristic. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 34, 39, 42"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with moderate dementia and limited-stage small cell lung carcinoma had a partial response to initial chemotherapy and radiation. He now needs help with most daily activities. His family asks about radiating his brain to prevent metastases. Which therapy should be avoided?","choices":["Brain magnetic resonance imaging","Prophylactic cranial irradiation","Chest and abdomen computed tomography","Smoking cessation support"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prophylactic cranial irradiation is the usual adjuvant step for limited-stage disease after a complete or partial response, but it is not recommended in patients with poor performance status or impaired neurocognitive function. His dementia and dependence in daily activities are both reasons to avoid it. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 43"}]};
